---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const productJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Product",
  "name": "Yidu Red Chilli",
  "description": "Yidu red (益都红) is a low-heat, thick-fleshed sweet-type pepper from Shandong and Xinjiang, grown as a paprika raw material for its colour yield (ASTA) rather than heat.",
  "url": "https://drychillis.com/products/yidu-chilli/",
  "sku": "TENDA-YIDU-RED-CHILLI",
  "category": "Spices and Seasonings",
  "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": "Tenda Peppers" },
  "manufacturer": { "@id": "https://drychillis.com/#organization" },
  "additionalProperty": [
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Heat Level (SHU)", "value": "<1,000 sweet range (under 10,000 ceiling)" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Colour", "value": "High (thick flesh) — confirm ASTA with us" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Region", "value": "Shandong (Yidu/Qingzhou), Xinjiang" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Fruit Type", "value": "Thick-fleshed sweet-type pod" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Best For", "value": "Paprika powder, natural red colouring, oleoresin" }
  ]
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is Yidu red chilli?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yidu red (益都红) is a low-heat, thick-fleshed sweet-type pepper from Shandong and Xinjiang, grown as a paprika raw material for its colour yield (ASTA) rather than heat — heat sits under 10,000 SHU and is typically far lower, near sweet."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How hot is Yidu red?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Essentially not hot — under 1,000 SHU in the sweet working range, and never near the 10,000-SHU low-heat ceiling even in hotter regional selections. It is bought for colour, so heat is the spec you do not need to chase on this variety."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is Yidu red used for?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yidu red is milled into low-heat paprika powder for natural red colouring, spice blends and paprika oleoresin (E160c) extraction. Its thick flesh gives a high colour yield per kilo, which is why it is a paprika raw material rather than a heat variety."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What ASTA colour value does Yidu red give?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A single published ASTA figure for the fresh pepper is not fixed — the colour value of the finished powder depends on variety selection, de-seeding and milling. Yidu red is high-colour (thick flesh), but confirm your target ASTA window with us rather than assuming a number."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is Yidu red different from Xinjiang sweet pepper?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Both are low-heat, high-colour paprika peppers. Xinjiang sweet pepper is the benchmark for very high colour at near-zero heat (sweet paprika under 500 SHU); Yidu red is a slightly fuller-bodied low-heat option with thick flesh and clear colour. Choose Xinjiang for pure colour, Yidu red for colour with a touch more body."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Yidu Red Chilli — Low-Heat Colour Pepper | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Yidu red (益都红) — low-heat, thick-fleshed sweet-type pepper from Shandong & Xinjiang for paprika powder and natural red colouring. China manufacturer."
  jsonLd={[productJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  multilingual={false}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/">Products</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/paprika-powder/">Paprika Powder</a> &rsaquo; Yidu Red
    </nav>
    <h1>Yidu Red Chilli</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A low-heat, thick-fleshed sweet-type pepper grown for colour — milled into low-heat paprika
      powder for natural red colouring, spice blends and paprika oleoresin (E160c) extraction.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Yidu red (益都红) is a colour pepper, not a heat pepper. Heat sits under 10,000 SHU and is typically near sweet; what you buy is colour yield (ASTA), which comes from its thick flesh. Specify Yidu red by ASTA and mesh — not by SHU.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Yidu red chilli?</h2>
    <p>
      Yidu red is a low-heat, thick-fleshed pepper traditionally associated with the Yidu (Qingzhou)
      area of Shandong and now also grown in Xinjiang's colour-pepper belt. It is a paprika raw
      material: grown and milled for its red colour, not its heat. On the heat scale it sits at the
      bottom — under 10,000 SHU and, in the sweet working range, under 1,000 SHU.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td><td>&lt;1,000 sweet range (under 10,000 ceiling)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Colour</strong></td><td>High (thick flesh) — confirm ASTA with us</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td>Shandong (Yidu/Qingzhou), Xinjiang</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Fruit type</strong></td><td>Thick-fleshed sweet-type pod</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Paprika powder, natural red colouring, oleoresin</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="data-note">
      Exact SHU and ASTA for Yidu red are not published as a single fixed figure and vary by
      selection and region (LOW confidence) — confirm your target values with us.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Yidu red used for?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Low-heat paprika powder</li>
      <li>Natural red colouring in seasoning and processed food</li>
      <li>Spice blends and marinades where colour matters and heat must stay low</li>
      <li>Paprika oleoresin (natural red food colour E160c) extraction</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Yidu Red vs Xinjiang Sweet Pepper vs Wangdu</h2>
    <p>
      Three low-heat, high-colour peppers, bought for different balances of colour and body:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Variety</th><th>Heat</th><th>Colour role</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Xinjiang sweet pepper</strong></td><td>&lt;500 SHU</td><td>Benchmark high colour, near-zero heat</td><td>Pure colour, sweet paprika</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Yidu red</strong></td><td>&lt;1,000 (to &lt;10,000)</td><td>High colour, thick flesh, fuller body</td><td>Low-heat paprika with aroma</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Wangdu</strong></td><td>&lt;10,000</td><td>Colour with mild warmth</td><td>Low-heat paprika blends</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The practical rule: if your spec demands zero perceptible heat, Xinjiang sweet pepper is the
      default. If you want colour with a touch more body and a faint chilli note, Yidu red is the
      fuller-bodied choice.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What most guides skip about Yidu red</h2>
    <p>
      Most guides call Yidu red "a sweet pepper" and stop there. What they skip is the economics:
      Yidu red is bought for <strong>colour yield per kilo</strong>, and because it is thick-fleshed,
      that yield can differ by a factor of two between grades and selections. Two lots of "Yidu red"
      can both look red and yet differ sharply in how far a given weight will colour your product.
      That is why the right order names a target ASTA window — not "red colour".
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify Yidu red</h2>
    <p>
      Name the variety, then the finished form (paprika powder is the standard), target ASTA colour
      value, mesh (60–80 standard) and moisture (under 13%). Heat is a non-issue at this end of the
      scale, so put your spec effort into colour and particle size instead.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Yidu red chilli?</h3>
      <p>Yidu red (益都红) is a low-heat, thick-fleshed sweet-type pepper from Shandong and Xinjiang, grown as a paprika raw material for colour yield (ASTA) — heat sits under 10,000 SHU and is typically near sweet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How hot is Yidu red?</h3>
      <p>Essentially not hot — under 1,000 SHU in the sweet working range and never near the 10,000-SHU ceiling even in hotter selections. It is bought for colour, not heat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Yidu red used for?</h3>
      <p>Yidu red is milled into low-heat paprika powder for natural red colouring, spice blends and paprika oleoresin (E160c) extraction. Its thick flesh gives a high colour yield per kilo.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What ASTA colour value does Yidu red give?</h3>
      <p>A single published ASTA figure for the fresh pepper is not fixed — the finished powder's value depends on selection, de-seeding and milling. Confirm your target ASTA window with us rather than assuming a number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How is Yidu red different from Xinjiang sweet pepper?</h3>
      <p>Both are low-heat, high-colour paprika peppers. Xinjiang sweet pepper is the benchmark for very high colour at near-zero heat; Yidu red is a fuller-bodied low-heat option with thick flesh. Choose Xinjiang for pure colour, Yidu red for colour with more body.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Request Yidu Red Quotation</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/products/paprika-powder/" style="margin-left:8px;">Paprika Powder →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
